|Unsupervised Learning|

Imagine a bank has 10,000 customer records, but it does not have labels describing the types of customers.
It wants to discover groups based on customer behavior.

For example, the algorithm might discover these groups:
- Group A: Customers who make frequent transactions.
- Group B: Customers who maintain high balances but transact less often.
- Group C: Customers who make frequent small transactions.

Important unsupervised-learning techniques:

A. Clustering
Groups similar data points together.

Common algorithms:
- K-Means
- Hierarchical Clustering
- DBSCAN

B. Dimensionality reduction
Reduces the number of variables while trying to preserve useful information.

Common techniques:
- PCA (Principal Component Analysis)
- t-SNE (often used for visualization)
- UMAP (often used for visualization and dimensionality reduction)

C. Anomaly detection
Identifies observations that differ substantially from typical patterns. 
Some anomaly-detection methods are unsupervised, while others use labels or semi-supervised approaches.

Supervised vs. unsupervised: a banking example
Scenario	                                     |Suitable approach
Historical transactions are labeled fraud/not  |fraud	Supervised classification
You want to group customers by spending behavior|	Unsupervised clustering
You want to find unusual transactions without fraud labels	|Unsupervised anomaly detection can help
